      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\sin(x + y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\\) for all \\(x \\in [0, \\pi]\\) and \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), we can start by using the angle addition formula for sine. The angle addition formula states that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x + y) = \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nGiven this, we can rewrite the inequality as:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this inequality to set all terms to one side:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\(\\sin(x)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x)(\\cos(y) - 1) + \\sin(y)(\\cos(x) - 1) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nFor this inequality to hold for all \\(x\\) in the interval \\([0, \\pi]\\), we need to consider the behavior of \\(\\cos(y - x)\\) over this interval. \n\nRecall the trigonometric identity:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(y - x) = \\cos(y)\\cos(x) + \\sin(y)\\sin(x)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y\\) is uniformly distributed in \\([0, \\pi]\\), we look for a scenario that allows \\(\\cos(y - x)\\) to be at least zero for all \\(x\\). This leads us to analyze when \\(\\cos(y - x)\\) is non-negative:\n\n1. When exactly zero:\n   \\[\n   \\cos(x) = 0\n   \\]\n\nAt \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + y) \\leq \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\cos(y) = \\pi\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\cos(y)\\) cannot be \\(\\pi\\) as its value must be within \\([-1, 1]\\).\n\n2. When exactly \\(1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos(x) = 1\n   \\]\n\nAt \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = \\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\sin(\\pi + y) \\leq \\sin(\\pi) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin(y) \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), this implies:\n\\[\n0 \\leq y \\leq \\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, combining these conditions, we can simplify the range of \\(y\\) by focusing on a specific interval where \\(\\cos(x - y) \\geq 0\\) for all \\(x \\in [0, \\pi]\\).\n\nWe investigate when exactly \\(\\cos(y - x) = 1\\):\n\\[\ny - x = 0 \\implies x = y, \\quad x \\in [0, \\pi]\n\\]\n\nThis rearrangement means the inequality is satisfied if \\(y\\) recreates \\(x\\) at these uniform intervals. Therefore, regardless of the range of \\(x\\), the only permissible \\(y\\) also falls within an \\(x\\) congruent interval:\n\nConsequently:\n\\[\ny = 2 \\zap{minute domain} (0,\\pi) = 2 \\times \\left[\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\n\\]\n\nOr:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
