      "text": "We know $\\sin(x+y)=\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\cos(x)\\sin(y)$.  Substituting these terms into the inequality, the desired inequality is $$\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y).$$The sine function is increasing on the interval $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, and the inequality holds on that interval.\n\nIf $y \\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$, then adding $y$ to $\\sin(x)$ accounts for a (positive) increment of $y$, but subtracting from $\\sin(x+y)$ accounts for a (negative) decrease of $y$.  Therefore, the inequality holds for all $y \\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi\\right)$.\n\nSince $\\sin(x)=0$ for all integer values of x and $\\sin(\\pi)=0$, we see that the inequality holds for all $y \\in [0,\\pi]$.\n\nIn conclusion, $\\boxed{[0,\\pi]}$.\nI think this problem comes out of a convenience.\n\nThe domain you're working over has a minimum of 1009 possibilities, but those possible roots require $\\frac{y}{2}\\leq x+y\\leq x\\leq\\frac{\\pi+y}{2}$, and adding these together gives you approximately $745$. There are some roots with one possible solution, but you would still have $130$ possibilities to confirm.",
